{
 "id": "095-tencere-tava-orkestrasi",
 "lang": "en",
 "title": "The Spoon and Bowl Orchestra",
 "theme": "Making rhythms with kitchen things, listening and repeating in turns",
 "blurb": "On a rainy day, Grandpa sets up an orchestra in the kitchen with steel bowls, unbreakable plates and spoons. Ali and Zeynep make up their own rhythms, but when they both play at once, the rhythms get lost. After a breath, the children find the echo game and taking turns all by themselves.",
 "voiceNames": {
  "dede": "Grandpa",
  "ali": "Ali",
  "zeynep": "Zeynep"
 },
 "pages": [
  {
   "text": "Rain tapped on the window: pit, pat, pit. Grandpa was stirring soup in the kitchen. Ali and Zeynep watched the drops run down. “We're so bored, Grandpa!” they said. Grandpa tapped his spoon gently on a glass: ting! “Listen now. There's an orchestra hiding in this kitchen!”",
   "hits": {
    "dede": {
     "say": "Ting! The glass sings too!"
    },
    "ali": {
     "say": "The rain just won't stop!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "We're so bored, Grandpa!"
    },
    "corba": {
     "say": "Mmm, lentil soup!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch Grandpa!",
   "ask": "Which things in the kitchen make a sound, do you think? Name one and make its sound!"
  },
  {
   "text": "Grandpa opened the cupboard. He put two steel bowls and two unbreakable plates on the floor. And a glass full of water. “This one you only touch gently!” he said. Ali and Zeynep each took a spoon. “Everyone find your own rhythm!” said Grandpa.",
   "hits": {
    "dede": {
     "say": "Everyone find your own rhythm!"
    },
    "ali": {
     "say": "I chose the bowl!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "And I chose the plate!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "Five instruments! Each one sounds different!",
    "msg": "Five instruments!"
   },
   "hint": "Count and listen!",
   "ask": "Which one goes tock, and which one goes clang? Make the sound!"
  },
  {
   "text": "Ali hit the bowl: tock tock tock! Zeynep hit the plate: clang clang clang! Then they both played at once. Each one wanted to be heard: faster, louder! Clitter-clatter, clang-a-bang! Zeynep screwed up her face. Ali's head throbbed. It was all too much for both of them.",
   "hits": {
    "ali": {
     "say": "My rhythm got lost! My head is throbbing!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Too much noise! It's too much!"
    },
    "dede": {
     "say": "Hmm… There must be a way."
    }
   },
   "predict": {
    "q": "What do you think they'll do now?",
    "res": "Let's find out!",
    "opts": [
     {
      "l": "Let's take turns"
     },
     {
      "l": "Let's listen first"
     }
    ]
   },
   "hint": "Touch Ali!",
   "ask": "What happens in your body when it's very noisy? What do you do?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Grandpa raised his hand: “Let's take a breath first.” In through the nose, out through the mouth: fwooo. Zeynep had an idea. “Grandpa, you play! We'll repeat it like an echo!” Grandpa played: tock, clang, ting! Ali and Zeynep played the same.",
   "hits": {
    "dede": {
     "say": "Zeynep's idea is brilliant!"
    },
    "ali": {
     "say": "Fwooo… Ah, that's better!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "You play, we'll repeat it!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "Tock, clang, ting! You played the same!",
    "msg": "You repeated the rhythm!"
   },
   "hint": "Tock, clang, ting!",
   "ask": "Why do you think Grandpa had them take a breath first? Let's blow fwooo together, then play with our hands: tock, clang, ting!"
  },
  {
   "text": "“Now it's our turn!” said Ali. “First Zeynep, then me.” Zeynep made up a slow rhythm: clang… ting… tock. Like a raindrop! Ali made up a fast rhythm: tock tock tock, clang! Grandpa repeated them both like an echo.",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Clang… ting… tock. Slowly!"
    },
    "ali": {
     "say": "First Zeynep, then me!"
    },
    "dede": {
     "say": "I'm repeating it too!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "Clang… ting… tock! Zeynep's slow rhythm!",
    "msg": "You repeated it like an echo!"
   },
   "hint": "Clang, ting, tock!",
   "ask": "Would you make up a slow rhythm or a fast one? Play it for us, and we'll repeat it!"
  },
  {
   "text": "The rain stopped, the sun came out. But the orchestra didn't stop! One slow, one fast; one Ali, one Zeynep, one Grandpa. Then all together: tock, clang, ting! This time, no rhythm got lost. “Shall we play tomorrow too?” said Zeynep. Grandpa winked: “Let's have our soup first!”",
   "hits": {
    "ali": {
     "say": "Tock, clang, ting! Hooray!"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Shall we play tomorrow too?"
    },
    "dede": {
     "say": "Let's have our soup first!"
    },
    "corba": {
     "say": "Enjoy your soup!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch the soup!",
   "ask": "What did you like best about this orchestra? Which things at home would you make music with?"
  }
 ],
 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "MÇB3 Musical Playing (field skill) · MÇB learning outcome “Playing the sounds and musical works they hear”: Listens to the “tock, clang, ting” rhythm Grandpa plays and repeats it in the same order on bowl, plate and glass; accompanies differences in fast and slow tempo with percussion (kitchen) instruments.",
   "MYB5 Musical Creativity (field skill) · MYB learning outcome “Creating musical products based on their musical experiences and presenting them”: Makes up their own slow or fast rhythm with kitchen things and presents it by playing it to friends.",
   "E3.3 Creativity and D11 Freedom: Creates instruments from everyday objects; while using the freedom to choose their own rhythm, notices that others have the right to be heard too.",
   "SDB2.2 Cooperation: While playing together, they find the idea of taking turns themselves and listen to each other; by listening first and then repeating, they build a shared rhythm."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Count the things in the kitchen that make a sound together: can we make music with them?",
   "Before tapping the table, tap your knees slowly, then quickly; name the difference together: slow, fast."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 2, as you count the instruments, copy each one's sound with your mouth: tock, clang, ting.",
   "On page 3, ask how the noise feels in the body; accept every answer to the prediction question.",
   "On page 4, breathe with Grandpa (in through the nose, fwooo out through the mouth), then repeat the rhythm on the screen and with your hands.",
   "On page 5, play Zeynep's slow rhythm and Ali's fast rhythm in turn; ask who came up with the idea of taking turns."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Why did Ali and Zeynep feel overwhelmed? What did they do to feel better?",
   "Whose idea was the echo game? Who suggested taking turns? Why did it work?",
   "Put the events in order with the pictures: they both played at once → they took a breath → they played in turns."
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Kitchen Orchestra Echo Game",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "Plastic or metal bowls, unbreakable plates, wooden and metal spoons, dried beans in a plastic bottle (tightly closed)",
   "adim": [
    "The children sit in a circle; each child picks a kitchen item and a spoon. No breakable glass or china is used.",
    "For children who are sensitive to sound, offer a ‘whisper rhythm’ option (with fingertips); a child who prefers can be the listener/conductor.",
    "The teacher plays a short three-beat rhythm (e.g. tock-tock-clang); the children listen first, then repeat it all together.",
    "In turn, each child makes up their own slow or fast rhythm; the group becomes the “echo” and repeats it.",
    "For one round, everyone plays at the same time; then talk about “In which one could we hear the rhythms?”",
    "To close, the whole class starts the same rhythm slowly and speeds up, then slows to a whisper and stops."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Which kitchen things did Grandpa use for his orchestra?",
   "What did Ali and Zeynep do when it got too much?",
   "Which rhythm would you like to make up? Play it for me, and I'll repeat it!"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Echo Orchestra at Home",
   "d": "Pick two unbreakable things from the kitchen (a plastic bowl, a wooden spoon, a saucepan lid). Take turns making up short rhythms and repeat each other like an echo; play one slow, one fast. When you finish, put the things back together. At home, use a steel or plastic cup instead of a glass one."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “The Spoon and Bowl Orchestra.” On a rainy day, while cooking soup in the kitchen, Grandpa set up an orchestra with his grandchildren out of steel bowls, unbreakable plates and spoons. The children made up their own rhythms; when they both played at once it was too much, so they took a breath; then they suggested the echo game and taking turns themselves. At home you can play the “listen and repeat” game with unbreakable kitchen things."
 },
